# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Divergence log. One entry per file marked "diverged" above.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# cosi/sidecar/driver.go
#   Added NewDriverInfo(driverReportedInfo, client), which takes an already-built
#   cosiproto.ProvisionerClient instead of a grpc.ClientConnInterface.
#
#   Why: upstream's ValidateAndSetDriverConnectionInfo builds the client itself
#   from a connection, which assumes the driver is a separate process. Trident
#   serves COSI in-process, so it has a client and no connection. The validation
#   it performs is not optional -- an invalid driver name means the reconcilers
#   silently match no objects -- and both validateDriverName and
#   validateAndParseProtocols are unexported.
#
#   Shape: ValidateAndSetDriverConnectionInfo was reduced to a one-line wrapper
#   that calls NewDriverInfo. No validation is duplicated and no upstream
#   behaviour changed, so a future upstream edit to either check applies to both
#   paths. Reverting the divergence is deleting NewDriverInfo and inlining its
#   body back into the original function.
#
#   Upstreamable: yes, and worth offering -- any in-process COSI driver hits this.
#
# cosi/sidecar/bucket.go
#   Replaced the deletion stub in reconcile() with a single call to r.reconcileDelete,
#   implemented in cosi/sidecar/bucket_delete.go.
#
#   Why: upstream carries "TODO: deletion logic" and does not deprovision buckets. Worse
#   than a no-op, the stub removes the protection finalizer and *then* returns an error,
#   so the Bucket object is deleted while the backend bucket is orphaned with nothing in
#   the cluster naming it. Confirmed on a live cluster, not inferred: kubectl delete
#   bucketclaim reported success and the driver was never called. Trident implements
#   deletion (Decision 4), so this path cannot ship as upstream wrote it.
#
#   Shape: the whole stub block collapses to one call. All the behaviour lives in
#   bucket_delete.go, which is not vendored, so the diff on upstream's most load-bearing
#   reconciler stays reviewable at a glance. Reverting the divergence is restoring the
#   stub block and deleting bucket_delete.go plus its test.
#
#   Upstreamable: not as-is. The semantics are NetApp decisions pending supportability
#   sign-off -- see design-docs/cosi/07-deletion-semantics.md -- and upstream would need
#   to make its own. The ordering fix alone (never drop the finalizer before the driver
#   confirms) is worth reporting upstream regardless, since the current stub leaks.
#
# cosi/controller/bucketclaim.go
#   In reconcileDelete, changed the log field bucketDeletionPolicy from the hardcoded
#   constant BucketDeletionPolicyRetain to bucket.Spec.DeletionPolicy.
#
#   Why: upstream sets this log field to the literal Retain constant regardless of the
#   bucket's actual policy, so every log line in the delete path -- including the ones on
#   the Delete branch, actively deprovisioning a bucket -- claims bucketDeletionPolicy=Retain.
#   Found live on 2026-09-28 (design-docs/cosi/19-grant-revoke-access-test-plan.md) while
#   reading delete-path logs to diagnose an unrelated timing question; cosmetic only, no
#   behavior depends on this field, but it actively misleads anyone reading the log.
#
#   Shape: one identifier changed on one line. Nothing else in the function moved.
#   Reverting the divergence is restoring the literal constant.
#
#   Upstreamable: yes -- straightforward one-line fix, no NetApp-specific behavior involved.
#
#   Also, in SetupWithManager, the Bucket watch's predicate changed from AnyCreate() to
#   ctrlpredicate.Or(AnyCreate(), AnyDelete()). It must stay a single Or: WithPredicates ANDs
#   its arguments.
#
#   Why: with Create only, a Bucket's delete event is dropped, so a deleting claim notices its
#   Bucket is gone only on its error backoff (5 ms doubling, 1000 s cap). Upstream never sees
#   this because its Bucket stub drops the finalizer at once; Trident's deletion can refuse for
#   minutes (non-empty bucket, unreachable backend), after which the claim stayed Terminating
#   for 5-11 min with its Bucket already gone (design-docs/cosi/22-pr-2856-test-results.md,
#   BD-4 and M8-repro).
#
#   Shape: one line changed. Reverting the divergence is restoring AnyCreate().
#
#   Upstreamable: yes -- upstream main still carries the AnyCreate()-only watch and the
#   "return nil when Bucket watcher is set up" TODOs.
#
# cosi/sidecar/bucketaccess.go
#   Added the SkipRegrantWhenGranted field to BucketAccessReconciler and, in reconcile(),
#   a check after reserveAccessSecrets that returns before DriverGrantBucketAccess when the
#   access is already granted: readyToUse, accountID set, Key/S3, and every access Secret
#   already holds both S3 credentials. A status error is ignored, since reconcile records
#   transient lookup failures there too, and is cleared when the grant is skipped. The
#   predicate lives in cosi/sidecar/bucketaccess_regrant.go, which is not vendored.
#
#   Why: upstream calls DriverGrantBucketAccess on every reconcile, including every resync
#   and every controller restart, and overwrites the Secrets with the response. The proto
#   asks drivers to treat that as idempotent, but StorageGRID only reveals a secret key when
#   it is minted, so the driver can only answer by deleting and re-creating keys. Every
#   restart therefore rotated keys under running consumers.
#
#   Cost: a granted access is no longer re-checked against the backend, so a key deleted
#   out of band is not repaired until the access is re-created or one of its Secrets loses
#   a credential (either makes the check fail and the driver is called again). The check
#   is per BucketAccess, so multiple accesses to one bucket are unaffected.
#
#   Shape: the field defaults to false, so upstream behaviour and the vendored
#   bucketaccess_test.go are unchanged; cosi/manager.go opts in. Reverting the divergence
#   is deleting the field, the four-line check, bucketaccess_regrant.go and its test.
#
#   Upstreamable: not as-is. The real fix is in the protocol (a way for the driver to say
#   "unchanged, keep the existing credentials"); this is a stopgap until then.
